window.docContents[232].resolve({"/Parabolic-Equations/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Classical-and-Weak-Heat-Flow--Variational-evolution":{"contents":"Galerkin existence, the Lions–Magenes representative, the energy estimate, and\nuniqueness hold for the Dirichlet heat equation on any bounded open set\n(); boundary smoothness enters\nonly when elliptic regularity is applied to almost every spatial slice. For\ntime-dependent divergence-form operators with a uniformly elliptic principal\npart and bounded lower-order terms, the same conclusions hold after a Gårding\nshift, provided the form  is almost everywhere strongly\nmeasurable and uniformly bounded in operator norm\n(). On  for any\nopen set , the Dirichlet heat semigroup is constructed by\nHille–Yosida ().\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Classical and Weak Heat Flow","header":"6.8.1.2. Variational evolution","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Classical-and-Weak-Heat-Flow--Variational-evolution"},"/Related-Work/#PDEAtlas--Related-Work--Regularity___-random-media___-and-fluid-flow":{"contents":"Scott Armstrong and Julia Kempe's formalization of De Giorgi–Nash–Moser theory\nproves regularity from a weak formulation\n. For uniformly elliptic divergence-form\nequations with bounded measurable coefficients, their development in dimensions\nat least three establishes local boundedness, weak Harnack and Harnack\ninequalities, and interior Hölder regularity. The mathematical gain is the passage\nfrom a weak formulation to pointwise regularity without assuming smooth\ncoefficients. Their supporting Sobolev and weak-solution infrastructure overlaps\nwith the foundations needed throughout PDE formalization.\n\nArmstrong and Kuusi's CoarseGraining development extends this direction to\nquantitative stochastic homogenization\n. Its public comparison\ntheorem is stated for uniformly elliptic, isotropic coefficient laws. Their\nSuperdiffusion formalization treats results from work with Bou-Rabee on random\nincompressible flows ,\nfor a model with specified properties.\n\nFor Navier–Stokes, Tomoki Uda's Leray–Hopf project reports global weak-existence\ntheorems for the unforced equation on the three-dimensional torus and on\n, together with energy inequalities\n. Its public weak formulation uses\nseparated tests , and its existence theorems are for weak\nsolutions.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Related Work","header":"2.3. Regularity, random media, and fluid flow","id":"/Related-Work/#PDEAtlas--Related-Work--Regularity___-random-media___-and-fluid-flow"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Scope___-Trust___-and-Notes/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Scope___-Trust___-and-Notes--Relationship-to-Mathlib-Tactics":{"contents":"The architecture is familiar from Mathlib's reflected tactics.\nring reifies polynomial expressions and proves equality through a normal\nform; norm_num proves supported numerical propositions; positivity\nconstructs sign information. PDEAtlas adds a separate reflected language and\nsoundness layer for the continuous operations documented here. It reuses\nMathlib's analytic definitions and theorem statements rather than replacing\nthem.\n\nThe symbolic frontend supports a finite grammar and bounded searches.\nFor goals already handled by ring, norm_num, or positivity, use those tactics directly.\n\nMathlib's inclusion engine is another close comparison\n. It combines registered enclosure\nrepresentations, expression handlers, hypotheses, and subdivision. Its default\nand +kernel modes check the final computation in the kernel; its optional\n+native mode additionally trusts compiled evaluation. Its pinned\ndyadic_interval family and CEGA's FloatLib route are compared in the\nrelated work chapter, together with the proposed Mathlib\ncomputable-polynomial types and PDEAtlas's rational sparse lists.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Scope, Trust, and Notes","header":"10.10.1. Relationship to Mathlib Tactics","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Scope___-Trust___-and-Notes/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Scope___-Trust___-and-Notes--Relationship-to-Mathlib-Tactics"}});